## ~03:55 — Research: two additional TLS stacks (primary sources)
- BoringSSL: Kyber768 shipped since 2023 as X25519Kyber768Draft00;
  updated to the standard X25519MLKEM768 name/codepoint as IETF
  standardisation completed; Chrome 131+ negotiates it by default
  (postquantum.com RFC 10024 summary; systemshardening.com deployment
  guide). Standalone ML-KEM: ML-KEM-1024 only, per the IETF
  draft-ietf-tls-mlkem writeup's implementation list ("BoringSSL (1024)").
  Group-preference enforcement: NOT verified — labelled unavailable in
  the matrix (BoringSSL was not built/tested here).
- Go (crypto/tls): X25519MLKEM768 on by default from Go 1.24 (Feb 2025)
  when CurvePreferences is nil; GODEBUG tlsmlkem=0 disables it (only when
  nil); explicit CurvePreferences is used verbatim as the server's
  preference order; Go 1.27 allows explicit hybrid enablement even with
  the GODEBUG set (Go 1.24 release notes; Go stdlib crypto/tls docs;
  silo.pgsty.com quoting the stdlib doc comment). Standalone ML-KEM: none
  ("Go has no native ML-KEM TLS group", cryptoramic.com lab check Go 1.27;
  IETF writeup's standalone list omits Go). TLS 1.2: classical-only
  (RFC 10024 — ML-KEM groups are TLS 1.3 only).
- Also confirmed for the guide: the OpenSSL SSL_CTX_set1_curves(3SSL)
  documentation's flat-list vs tuple semantics (the mechanism behind
  F-1) and the SSL_CONF_cmd -serverpref option.

## ~04:05 — Operator regression script (operator_check.py)
- Wrote a generic, operator-runnable script (argparse: --host/--port/
  --openssl/--cafile) that runs two tests against ANY TLS 1.3 server:
  A (deferred keyshare: client supports the hybrid but predicts only a
  classical keyshare; PASS iff negotiated = X25519MLKEM768 and an HRR was
  seen) and B (control: hybrid keyshare predicted; PASS iff negotiated =
  X25519MLKEM768). Exit 0 iff all pass.
- First local run FAILED both configs (rc=1, no group): the runner
  started s_server with inherited stdin (EOF in this detached env), so
  the server closed every connection before the handshake — the same F-6
  stdin-EOF bug from cycle 5. Fixed by giving the server stdin from a
  held-open pipe (`< <(sleep 60)` in run_operator_check.sh).
- Second run (2026-11-06 ~04:10 WIB): tuple server ALL PASS (exit 0);
  flat server exit 1 — test A FAILS (negotiated X25519, no HRR) with the
  NOTE about the flat-list misconfiguration, test B passes. Exactly the
  expected behaviour. Outputs saved unedited:
  outputs/raw/operator_check_tuple.log (exit 0),
  outputs/raw/operator_check_flat.log (exit 1).
- Cleaned up the two `sleep 60` helper processes afterwards; no stray
  servers left.

